2026-06-15 12:27:58,235 articletxt/485.txt
2026-06-15 12:27:58,235 TITLE: Mechanical pretreatments of different agri-based feedstock in full-scale biogas plants under real operational conditions Title of processing article
2026-06-15 12:28:03,594 {
  "pretreatments": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "description": "Knife milling (KM): A mechanical pretreatment involving mobile milling elements mounted on a central shaft within a milling chamber. It applies forces to disaggregate solid particles, slice fibers, and produce small pieces through cutting, impact, and shear between the knives and a sieve. Applied to substrates with total solids >85%."
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "description": "Hammer milling (HM): A mechanical pretreatment using a rotor with attached hammers that impact material against a breaker plate. This action grinds fibers into long and thin pieces through impact and compression stresses. Applied to substrates with total solids between 20% and 85%."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "description": "Extrusion (EX): A mechanical pretreatment where raw materials are transported along a barrel by a driving screw. The material is subjected to friction heat, mixing, vigorous shearing due to pressure changes, and abrasion upon pressure release at the discharge end, causing structural changes and cell wall breakdown."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "description": "Shredding + Hydrodynamic Cavitation (SHC): A combined mechanical pretreatment involving a wet shredder with an integrated heavy material separator to homogenize fluids and protect downstream components, followed by a hydrodynamic cavitator. The cavitator uses a stator and rotor assembly to generate cavitation (formation, growth, and collapse of microbubbles) via high shear forces and extreme local temperature/pressure changes."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "description": "Wet sieving: A physical analysis/preparation step using a vibration screening machine to determine particle size distribution. It involves shaking substrate samples on screens to separate particles by diameter, used here to calculate specific surface area assuming spherical, non-porous particles."
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "description": "Leachate preparation for chemical analysis: A sample preparation method where 25 g of solid substrate are placed in 300 g of distilled water and shaken at 200 rpm for 30 minutes to allow soluble components to leach out. The resulting mixture is then filtered through a 0.45 μm filter for the determination of electrical conductivity (EC) and soluble chemical oxygen demand (sCOD)."
    }
  ]
}
