2026-06-15 12:29:16,866 TITLE: Improving biogas production from different parts of spruce tree using leading pretreatments Title of processing article
2026-06-15 12:29:22,975 {
  "pretreatments": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "description": "Phosphoric acid pretreatment: 1 g of dry substrate mixed with 8 mL of phosphoric acid (83–85.9 %) in a centrifuge tube and placed in a water bath at 50 °C for 50 min. Subsequently, 20 mL of cold acetone was added, followed by stirring and centrifugation at 3400 rpm for 20 min. The supernatant was separated, and the solid was washed with distilled water via centrifugation three times until pH 7.0 was reached. The pretreated solids were dried at 40 °C. The combined liquid fractions were heated to remove acetone and then frozen for storage as phosphoric acid-pretreatment liquor."
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "description": "Organosolv pretreatment: 20 g of dry substrate mixed with 160 mL of 75 % (v/v) ethanol solution containing 1 % (w/w based on dry weight) sulfuric acid as a catalyst. The mixture was placed in a stainless steel reactor and heated in an oil bath at 150 °C for 30 min, followed by cooling in an ice bath. The liquid and solid phases were separated by filtration. The solids were washed with distilled water to neutralize them, while the liquids from both pretreatments were used directly. Both solid and liquid fractions were dried at 40 °C."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "description": "Dilute sulfuric acid pretreatment: 20 g of dry substrate mixed with 200 g of 0.5 % (w/w) sulfuric acid solution. The mixture was transferred to a reactor and heated in an oil bath at 140 °C for 5 min, followed by cooling in an ice-water bath. The liquid and solid phases were separated by filtration. The solids were washed with distilled water to neutralize them, then dried at 40 °C. The liquid phase was used as pretreatment liquor."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "description": "Mechanical size reduction: Different parts of the spruce tree (leaves, branches, bark, wood, fruit) were individually cut into small pieces and milled to obtain chips smaller than 2 cm. The chips were dried at room temperature and screened to achieve a powder with a particle size between 295 and 833 µm (20 and 80 mesh)."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "description": "Inoculum adaptation: The inoculum was incubated at 37 °C for one week to consume nutrients. It was then adapted to lignocellulosic substrates by feeding every five days with 1 g VS per liter of a mixture of different spruce parts for three months prior to use."
    }
  ]
}
